
  Remove git worktrees, delete their local branches and their meta records. The remote branch is never touched.
  Without arguments, the wizard asks which worktrees to remove; several can be checked.
  A worktree on no branch is named by its path; a branch no worktree holds is deleted alone.

  A worktree that is locked, has uncommitted changes, holds commits no other branch nor remote
  holds or has an open pull request is unsafe: one unsafe worktree refuses the whole
  run before anything is removed, unless --force. --force lifts that refusal only: it still
  asks to confirm, unless --yes.

  Each worktree is removed in turn: the jobs it runs stopped first (one that will not stop keeps
  it, unless --force), then its on_clean hooks: a failing hook keeps it. A failure
  does not stop the others. The children a removal leaves behind stay on their removed parent,
  named with the `wtm reparent` that moves them, unless --reparent-children moves them onto the
  nearest worktree that stays, else the base branch.

  The data a worktree made in shared services (a database, a bucket) is dropped once it is
  removed, its claims let go of after the last removal. A service that is down cannot drop it:
  it is owed until the service next starts (run up or prune drops it then), unless --drop-data
  starts the service to drop it now. --keep-data keeps it all.

  Usage:
    wtm-next clean [worktree...] [flags]

  Examples:
    # Pick the worktrees to remove
    wtm clean

    wtm clean feat/login

    # Several at once, no prompts; their children move onto the nearest survivor
    wtm clean feat/login feat/signup --yes --reparent-children

    # See the plan first, then apply exactly that plan
    wtm clean feat/login --dry-run
    wtm clean feat/login --expect-fingerprint <hash>

    # For a script or an agent, with the v2 JSON envelope
    wtm clean feat/login --yes --output json

  Flags:
        --ask                         Ask again the questions this repository remembers an answer to, to change or forget it
        --drop-data                   Start a shared service that is down to drop the namespaces the removed worktrees made in it now, rather than once it next starts
    -n, --dry-run                     Show the plan and its fingerprint, apply nothing
        --expect-fingerprint string   Apply only if the facts still have this fingerprint, the one --dry-run printed
        --force                       Remove a worktree that is locked, has uncommitted changes, unpushed commits or an open pull request, or whose jobs would not stop
    -h, --help                        help for clean
        --keep-data                   Keep the namespaces the removed worktrees made in shared services (databases, buckets): nothing is dropped
        --output string               Output format: text or json (default "text")
        --reparent-children           Move the children a removed worktree leaves behind onto its nearest surviving ancestor, else the base branch
    -y, --yes                         Run unattended: no question, no confirmation (worktrees required; children stay orphaned unless --reparent-children; an unsafe worktree still refuses the run unless --force)

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

